course: dictionary
lesson: 9371
title: Spoonerisms: swapping sounds
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A spoonerism is a slip of the tongue. You swap the first sounds of two words, often by accident, and the result is usually funny. Read the words slowly: «a well-boiled icicle» is what you say when you mean «a well-oiled bicycle».
explain: The sounds have changed places. The b from bicycle jumps onto oiled, so oiled becomes boiled, and bicycle loses its b and becomes icicle. Try swapping the first sounds of two words in your head. Does it make a silly phrase? You have found a spoonerism.
explain: The word comes from William Spooner, an Oxford teacher who was said to make this kind of slip often. Now the name is used for every swap like this, and people use spoonerisms in jokes and word games.
fact: A spoonerism is a slip of the tongue where the first sounds of two words swap places.
fact: Spoonerisms are often funny, so they are used in jokes and word games.
fact: «A well-boiled icicle» is a spoonerism for «a well-oiled bicycle».
fact: William Spooner was an Oxford teacher, and he was said to make this mistake often.
fact: Spoonerisms show that our brains plan the sounds of words before we say them.
exercise: What is a spoonerism? | A word that rhymes with another word | *A slip of the tongue where the first sounds of two words swap places | A spelling mistake on a page | A long word for a kind of bicycle
exercise: «A blushing crow» is a spoonerism for which phrase? | a bushy brow | *a crushing blow | a blushing bride | a crowing rooster
exercise: Why is it called a spoonerism? | It is named after a spoon used at dinner | *It is named after William Spooner, an Oxford teacher | It is named after a town called Spooner | It is named after a famous bicycle
test: In «a well-boiled icicle», which words had their first sounds swapped? | Well and boiled | Icicle and well | *Oiled and bicycle | Boiled and icicle
test: Why do many people enjoy spoonerisms? | They are a type of long, serious poem | *They are funny, and they show how our minds plan sounds | They only appear in science books | They are funny and make good word games
exam: Which sentence is a spoonerism? | «She ran fast to the shop» | «The cat sat on the mat» | «A big red ball» | *«Let me sew you to your seat» for «Let me show you to your seat»
